Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
it's always important when you're doing  stories on education to reflect that  things are going to be different in  different parts of the United Kingdom  what's happening in Wales and how  worried are you about children being out  of the classroom for so long well as  from tomorrow all schools across Wales  our van in Anglesey where we have a  local outbreak of coronavirus will go  back or children will have an  opportunity to check in to catch up and  to prepare for the summer and into the  autumn and we are very keen indeed to do  that because of our anxieties about  vulnerable children about children who  are moving from primary to secondary  school and of course in Wales we have  large numbers of children who are taught  through the medium of Welsh but who may  not hear that much Welsh at home it's  interesting isn't because you're  obviously taking the view that you're  trying to get all schools back into the  classroom children's right back into the  classroom at least for limited periods  rotating the children in school each day  it goes against some of the advice  that's given from the UK government  saying that teachers should try and  stick to one group of students where  possible to avoid being overexposed do  you worried that it could be putting  teachers at risk by this plan no we  worked very hard indeed with our trade  union colleagues and our local education  authorities they had more than three  weeks notice of our intention to reopen  schools and a huge effort has gone on by  teachings teachers and non teaching  staff to make sure that schools will be  safe safe for teachers say for students  I know hundreds of conversations across  Wales between parents and head teachers  looking to see what has been put in  place we are confident that we can do  this safely and we are confident that we  are doing the right thing for the  benefit of children it was really  interesting going to talk to the head  teacher at the school in Crawley this  week he was saying that new teachers can  do everything they can to reach out to  children they know a vulnerable but of  course if some are disengaged from  education it might be very difficult to  get them to re-engage unless they get  back in the classroom what kind of in  do you think it you will see on these  children if they are away from the  classroom so long well one of the  reasons why we working to reopen all  schools is that we've learned something  from the last three months we've had 450  schools in Wales open throughout the  pandemic for vulnerable children and  children of key workers what we're  finding is is that if you're a  vulnerable child and your school is open  you tend to go there but if you're asked  to go to a different school where  teachers you may not know when children  you may not be familiar with a journey  or not used to making then you tend not  to go there so by reopening all schools  we think we have a real chance to make  sure that those children we are most  concerned about come back to the school  door and then we will be able to have  that face-to-face contact with them talk  to their parents make a plan not all  children are in the position of having  all the resources they need at home and  our efforts are really concentrated on  making sure that we reach those we need  to reach the most and by opening all  schools we think that's a really  significant way in which we can make  that happen it's very interesting  observation actually you can see why  parents can be concerned about you know  their children particularly younger  children re settling in a new  environment in times already you know  very different to normality for them now  in England pubs restaurants and similar  venues it can be reopening from the  fourth of July what's happening in Wales  and do you think that Boris Johnson is  moving too quickly well we are planning  with our hospitality sector to see if we  can safely reopen outdoor hospitality  here in Wales either series are very  good conversations with the sector last  week and we hope to conclude those  conversations of the first half of next  week but we will continue to do things  in the way we've done things in Wales  carefully cautiously one step at a time  we put our efforts into planning first  and making the announcement second not  making an announcement and then thinking  about how you can make those things  happen  so do you think that's what the UK  government the Boris Johnson their  government's been doing well my concerns  with the UK government are sometimes  less with the substance where I think we  are all broadly still trying to do the  same things I'm more concerned at the  messaging the way things are described  here in Wales I am very keen to continue  to say to Welsh citizens coronavirus has  not gone away it remains something that  kills people in Wales every day if you  don't stay on top of it you will see  things going backwards and everything  that we have done together thrown away  and that's a very different message I  think to the message across the border  where the message seems much more that  you know it's all over and you can go  back to doing everything as you did  before I think we're seeing around the  world how that leads to flare-ups of the  disease again so in substance I don't  think we're that far apart we give a  much more sober message to people in  Wales because we are still in a  coronavirus crisis interesting to hear  about your concerns and the messaging do  you think the Prime Minister is perhaps  too keen to give good news well you know  it's not for me to tell other people how  they should make decisions elsewhere as  I say ëwell here in Wales we have had  enormous social solidarity from people  in sticking to the 2-meter rule in doing  the right things I don't want them to  hear a message that some simply says  it's all over there is going to be  freedom Saturday where you can do as you  like  you can't not if you want to be careful  about your own health and the health of  other people this crisis is not over  it's not over in Wales and it's not over  across the whole of the United Kingdom  and that's what we need to be reminding  people of as we move together to restore  freedoms to people where we are  confident we have the Headroom to do so  I just want to look at some of your  track record on white Wales as well  particularly on testing now we know of  course how important testing is as we  ease our way out of lockdown and trying  to stop any flare-ups of the virus in  the future  something whales have struggled with  frankly no you set in a drop to target  if 5,000 tests a day in April last month  the Welsh secretary said that Wells had  fallen behind the other three nations on  testing and it was making progress with  this whole recovery process so much  slower what is happening with testing in  Wales and have you really got a grip on  the situation no I think we are  absolutely on top of testing here in  Wales we have thousands more tests  available in Wales today than we did  earlier in the disease we have  demonstrated this week where we've had  two major outbreaks in north west and  north east Wales but our test race for  tech system has worked very effectively  where we've been on top of those  outbreaks very quickly where we  mobilized mass testing where the results  are back within 24 hours where we  believe we are able to contain the  outbreaks so we don't yet see community  spread from them we may have had to  build up our system more slowly but I  think our system is now in very good  shape  and demonstrating every day but it is  working effectively

